1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,160
It is 2:00 AM.

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:09,010
No customers are online, no traffic, no
deploys, and our billing is climbing,

3
00:00:09,790 --> 00:00:12,159
not by a little, by multiples.

4
00:00:13,030 --> 00:00:14,520
I check the dashboards.

5
00:00:14,650 --> 00:00:15,939
Everything is green.

6
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:17,450
Latency is fine.

7
00:00:17,570 --> 00:00:18,619
Errors are fine.

8
00:00:19,020 --> 00:00:22,279
Nothing is broken, but
the money is leaving.

9
00:00:22,869 --> 00:00:26,839
So I start pulling threads
and slowly it lands.

10
00:00:27,189 --> 00:00:29,009
This is not a customer.

11
00:00:29,459 --> 00:00:31,049
This is not an attack.

12
00:00:31,349 --> 00:00:37,599
This is us, our own agents
working hard all night on nothing.

13
00:00:38,169 --> 00:00:43,120
I'm Yamini, senior delivery tech
lead with AWS Professional Services.

14
00:00:43,509 --> 00:00:48,759
I serve as the face of AWS to my
customers, helping them translate

15
00:00:48,779 --> 00:00:53,859
their business goals into actionable
strategies and measurable outcomes.

16
00:00:54,410 --> 00:00:58,999
And I have Kartik, who is a senior
technical account manager with us,

17
00:00:59,279 --> 00:01:04,649
who serves as a trusted advisor to
customers, aligning cloud solutions

18
00:01:04,879 --> 00:01:09,770
with their business goals and helping
them realize measurable value.

19
00:01:10,379 --> 00:01:16,619
Tonight, we want to tell you how a
system that looked perfectly healthy

20
00:01:16,970 --> 00:01:24,129
quietly ate its own budget, how we
found it, and the small set of fixes

21
00:01:24,199 --> 00:01:26,719
that would have stopped it in minutes.

22
00:01:27,719 --> 00:01:29,429
Rewind a few weeks.

23
00:01:29,829 --> 00:01:36,519
We were proud of this thing, a multi-agent
system that felt like magic in the demo.

24
00:01:37,459 --> 00:01:42,159
A goal comes in, one orchestrator
breaks it into pieces.

25
00:01:42,539 --> 00:01:45,799
Each piece goes to a specialist agent.

26
00:01:46,199 --> 00:01:50,849
Each specialist calls tools
to get real work done.

27
00:01:51,379 --> 00:01:56,959
On stage, it was fast, it was clever,
every light was green, and that,

28
00:01:57,199 --> 00:02:00,129
it turns out, is exactly the trap.

29
00:02:00,679 --> 00:02:02,649
Here's the thing about demos.

30
00:02:03,049 --> 00:02:08,259
A demo runs once, twice on a happy
path with everything healthy.

31
00:02:08,789 --> 00:02:10,679
Production is the opposite.

32
00:02:11,090 --> 00:02:18,519
Production runs that same flow millions of
times, and some fraction of those runs hit

33
00:02:18,649 --> 00:02:22,149
something slow or flaky or briefly down.

34
00:02:22,750 --> 00:02:25,249
The demo never shows you that.

35
00:02:25,659 --> 00:02:28,900
Production shows you nothing but that

36
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:35,300
Let us look at the shape of the system
because the shape is the whole story.

37
00:02:35,770 --> 00:02:39,140
One orchestrator spawns the workers.

38
00:02:39,490 --> 00:02:41,830
Those workers call tools.

39
00:02:42,220 --> 00:02:45,710
Some of these workers
spawn their own workers.

40
00:02:46,020 --> 00:02:47,440
That is fan-out.

41
00:02:47,940 --> 00:02:55,010
One request becomes three agents, three
become nine, nine can become more.

42
00:02:55,550 --> 00:03:00,890
And when everything succeeds on the
first try, fan-out is beautiful.

43
00:03:01,580 --> 00:03:02,920
It is parallelism.

44
00:03:03,210 --> 00:03:08,180
It is how you get speed and
coverage out of a hard problem.

45
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:11,360
But notice the hidden assumption.

46
00:03:11,819 --> 00:03:17,879
Fan-out is only cheap while almost
everything succeeds first try.

47
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:22,540
Hold on to that thought because
in a minute, we are going to

48
00:03:22,620 --> 00:03:27,689
break that assumption and watch
what the same beautiful shape

49
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:30,520
does when things start to fail.

50
00:03:32,260 --> 00:03:38,030
Now, layer in something every
production system has, retries.

51
00:03:38,420 --> 00:03:42,959
We had three separate layers
of them, and each one on its

52
00:03:43,020 --> 00:03:45,369
own was completely reasonable.

53
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:48,019
Layer one, the SDK.

54
00:03:48,479 --> 00:03:54,940
The AWS SDK retries failed calls
about three times with backoff.

55
00:03:55,479 --> 00:03:57,649
That is on by default.

56
00:03:57,979 --> 00:03:59,600
It is invisible to you.

57
00:04:00,039 --> 00:04:02,109
Layer two, our application.

58
00:04:02,530 --> 00:04:07,949
We wrapped calls in our own
resilience logic that also retried.

59
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:10,679
Sensible, also invisible.

60
00:04:11,229 --> 00:04:13,330
Layer three, the agent itself.

61
00:04:13,799 --> 00:04:21,209
When a call fails, the agent doesn't
just retry, it re-reasons, a whole

62
00:04:21,219 --> 00:04:27,519
new model turn, and often it spawns
a helper to deal with the problem.

63
00:04:28,159 --> 00:04:34,770
Each layer was added by good
engineers for a good reason, but

64
00:04:34,969 --> 00:04:37,149
nobody looked at them together.

65
00:04:37,569 --> 00:04:42,059
And when you stack them, you
do not add, you multiply.

66
00:04:42,880 --> 00:04:48,330
Three times three, and then an
agent spawns, and the whole thing

67
00:04:48,340 --> 00:04:50,780
starts again one level down.

68
00:04:51,780 --> 00:04:56,120
Let us make that concrete because
multiplication is abstract

69
00:04:56,280 --> 00:04:57,990
until you put money on it.

70
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:04,040
Now, these numbers are illustrative,
but they are the shape of what we saw.

71
00:05:04,420 --> 00:05:11,100
Normal spend, roughly $300 a day,
steady, predictable, then the storm.

72
00:05:11,780 --> 00:05:16,740
Overnight, it hit around
$7,000, call it 20 times normal.

73
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:23,570
And when we traced a single request,
one request, we found it had fanned out

74
00:05:23,620 --> 00:05:26,410
into roughly 4,000 downstream calls.

75
00:05:26,830 --> 00:05:30,050
Here's why that is different
from a normal outage.

76
00:05:31,220 --> 00:05:35,470
A classic retry adds one more pass.

77
00:05:35,770 --> 00:05:36,760
It is linear.

78
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:38,590
You barely notice.

79
00:05:39,060 --> 00:05:45,660
Agentic fan-out does not add, it
multiplies at every layer across

80
00:05:45,730 --> 00:05:51,560
every agent all at once, and
the punchline is the quiet part.

81
00:05:51,980 --> 00:05:54,450
The dependency never fully failed.

82
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:55,580
It stayed up.

83
00:05:55,890 --> 00:06:01,110
The only thing that fell
was over-- was the bill.

84
00:06:02,240 --> 00:06:07,470
Now, so what actually
set it out or set it off?

85
00:06:07,820 --> 00:06:10,170
It was not an outage.

86
00:06:10,470 --> 00:06:16,420
An outage would have been easier,
honestly, because an outage pages someone.

87
00:06:16,840 --> 00:06:24,080
What if what we had was even worse,
a dependency that was slow and

88
00:06:24,180 --> 00:06:29,530
intermittently failing, up enough
that no alarm fired, broken enough

89
00:06:29,590 --> 00:06:32,980
that about one call in five failed.

90
00:06:33,190 --> 00:06:36,120
One in five sounds survivable.

91
00:06:36,540 --> 00:06:38,930
On a single call path, it is.

92
00:06:39,210 --> 00:06:41,020
You retry, you move on.

93
00:06:41,170 --> 00:06:43,770
But we did not have a single call path.

94
00:06:43,770 --> 00:06:49,700
We had dozens of agents and three
layers of retry, and every one of

95
00:06:49,700 --> 00:06:55,930
them independently decided the same
thing at the same moment: try harder.

96
00:06:56,260 --> 00:07:02,449
One in five failures multiplied by
fan-out, multiplied by three retry layers,

97
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:06,480
all deciding to try harder in parallel.

98
00:07:06,890 --> 00:07:12,750
That is the trigger In a normal
system, a retry is linear.

99
00:07:13,140 --> 00:07:15,930
One call fails, you make one more call.

100
00:07:16,170 --> 00:07:19,040
The cost of failure is one extra pass.

101
00:07:19,370 --> 00:07:21,990
You can reason about it on a napkin.

102
00:07:22,350 --> 00:07:27,130
In an agentic system with fan-out
or retry compounds, follow

103
00:07:27,130 --> 00:07:29,610
the chain, a tool call fails.

104
00:07:29,860 --> 00:07:33,120
That triggers a new agentic turn.

105
00:07:33,670 --> 00:07:37,900
The agent re-reasons,
which burns the tokens.

106
00:07:38,180 --> 00:07:40,000
It reissues the call.

107
00:07:40,340 --> 00:07:42,550
It spawns a helper agent to help.

108
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:47,730
That helper has its own
retries and its own fan-out.

109
00:07:47,960 --> 00:07:53,060
So you are burning tokens from
all the re-reasoning, and you are

110
00:07:53,070 --> 00:07:58,550
burning infrastructure from all the
reinvocation at the same time, and

111
00:07:58,640 --> 00:08:04,980
every bit of it looks like healthy,
successful activity on your dashboards.

112
00:08:05,420 --> 00:08:11,130
Let us tell you what that silent
compounding looked like The next

113
00:08:11,130 --> 00:08:13,690
morning, the cost line was vertical.

114
00:08:14,200 --> 00:08:15,910
Not a bump, a wall.

115
00:08:16,380 --> 00:08:20,970
Multiplies of normal across
both tokens and infrastructure.

116
00:08:21,270 --> 00:08:25,440
And here is what made it
frightening rather than annoying.

117
00:08:25,780 --> 00:08:28,760
There was no traffic spike to point to.

118
00:08:29,290 --> 00:08:31,670
No new deploy to roll back to.

119
00:08:31,940 --> 00:08:33,430
No incident open.

120
00:08:33,430 --> 00:08:34,950
No alarm had fired.

121
00:08:35,020 --> 00:08:41,230
Every single one of our health signals
said the system was fine, because by

122
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:44,410
every technical definition, it was.

123
00:08:44,970 --> 00:08:46,710
Calls were succeeding.

124
00:08:46,880 --> 00:08:48,110
Errors were low.

125
00:08:48,280 --> 00:08:50,000
Latency was in range.

126
00:08:50,010 --> 00:08:56,230
So this stopped being a cost problem,
which is a thing you can usually

127
00:08:56,240 --> 00:09:00,420
use, just attribute and move on from.

128
00:09:00,890 --> 00:09:02,160
It became a mystery.

129
00:09:02,410 --> 00:09:06,640
Something was spending our money,
everything looked healthy, and

130
00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:09,190
we had no idea where to look.

131
00:09:10,960 --> 00:09:15,770
And it was genuinely hard to
find for a specific reason.

132
00:09:16,070 --> 00:09:18,630
The cost was not concentrated.

133
00:09:18,800 --> 00:09:25,190
It was smeared across dozens of
agents, each one doing a small,

134
00:09:25,230 --> 00:09:27,790
reasonable amount of extra work.

135
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:30,580
No single agent looked guilty.

136
00:09:30,940 --> 00:09:33,800
Worse, our error rate looked fine.

137
00:09:34,730 --> 00:09:35,660
Think about why.

138
00:09:35,870 --> 00:09:39,720
A call fails, we retry,
it eventually succeeds.

139
00:09:39,850 --> 00:09:44,210
To every metric we had,
that reads as success.

140
00:09:44,570 --> 00:09:48,880
The failures were hiding
inside our success numbers.

141
00:09:49,120 --> 00:09:51,850
The breakthrough was a mindset shift.

142
00:09:52,290 --> 00:09:56,180
We stopped counting errors
and started counting volume.

143
00:09:56,660 --> 00:10:00,800
Not did it succeed,
but how much did it do?

144
00:10:01,580 --> 00:10:08,130
Agent turns per request, tool calls
per request, and there it was.

145
00:10:08,400 --> 00:10:13,409
One request had fired
thousands of downstream calls.

146
00:10:13,620 --> 00:10:15,190
That was the fingerprint.

147
00:10:15,410 --> 00:10:21,694
That is when it clicked for the
culprit So who is the villain?

148
00:10:22,224 --> 00:10:23,644
It is not one thing.

149
00:10:24,164 --> 00:10:27,004
That is what took us long, so long.

150
00:10:27,464 --> 00:10:32,974
It is three things: nested
retries, fan-out, and no budget.

151
00:10:33,584 --> 00:10:36,254
Each one of those is ordinary.

152
00:10:36,864 --> 00:10:41,964
Each one is something you would find
in a healthy, well-built system.

153
00:10:42,684 --> 00:10:46,314
They are only dangerous in combination.

154
00:10:46,844 --> 00:10:50,744
And here's the uncomfortable,
important part.

155
00:10:51,194 --> 00:10:52,954
The system was not broken.

156
00:10:53,314 --> 00:10:54,904
Nothing malfunctioned.

157
00:10:55,144 --> 00:10:57,754
It was working exactly as designed.

158
00:10:58,374 --> 00:11:01,134
Every retry did its job.

159
00:11:01,274 --> 00:11:03,704
Every agent did its job.

160
00:11:04,004 --> 00:11:06,264
Every layer did its job.

161
00:11:06,474 --> 00:11:10,724
The problem was not a bug,
the problem was the design.

162
00:11:11,194 --> 00:11:18,074
The design was quietly, expensively
wrong, and it was wrong in a way

163
00:11:18,234 --> 00:11:21,444
that no single component could see.

164
00:11:22,054 --> 00:11:27,434
Now, I would like to hand over to my
co-speaker, Karthik Akula, to walk

165
00:11:27,434 --> 00:11:33,354
you through why this is an agentic
problem and what we instrumented

166
00:11:34,354 --> 00:11:35,474
Hey, thanks, Yamini.

167
00:11:35,874 --> 00:11:36,294
Hey, everyone.

168
00:11:36,294 --> 00:11:37,464
This is Karthik Akula.

169
00:11:37,524 --> 00:11:39,404
I'm a senior technical account manager.

170
00:11:39,894 --> 00:11:43,014
I'll walk you through the
rest of the portion from here.

171
00:11:43,804 --> 00:11:47,294
Let us sit with that because
it is the real lesson, and

172
00:11:47,294 --> 00:11:48,704
it is not a technical one.

173
00:11:49,264 --> 00:11:51,114
Nobody did anything wrong.

174
00:11:51,274 --> 00:11:54,394
The SDK retries were on by default.

175
00:11:54,714 --> 00:11:56,844
That is good engineering, right?

176
00:11:56,894 --> 00:12:01,124
The application wrapper was added for
resilience, and that is good engineering.

177
00:12:01,644 --> 00:12:05,564
The agent re-reasoning was
added to make it smarter, and

178
00:12:05,574 --> 00:12:07,044
that is good engineering too.

179
00:12:07,374 --> 00:12:09,654
Each decision was locally sensible.

180
00:12:09,704 --> 00:12:14,154
Each was made by a different team for
a good reason at a different time.

181
00:12:14,674 --> 00:12:18,534
The failure was that no one
owned retries end to end.

182
00:12:19,124 --> 00:12:24,004
Three lo-local optimum stacked
into one global failure mode.

183
00:12:24,284 --> 00:12:27,324
So the real fix is not
to find someone to blame.

184
00:12:27,674 --> 00:12:34,224
It is to create the owner, one budget, and
one witness for the whole request path.

185
00:12:34,654 --> 00:12:38,743
Someone whose job is the
sum, but not the part.

186
00:12:39,744 --> 00:12:44,334
You might be thinking retries
and fanouts are old news, right?

187
00:12:44,674 --> 00:12:45,394
And you're right.

188
00:12:45,474 --> 00:12:49,664
We have handled retries for decades,
so what is actually new here?

189
00:12:50,154 --> 00:12:54,914
The new thing here is the
new multiplier is reasoning.

190
00:12:55,244 --> 00:12:59,754
In a classic system, a retry
re-reruns a cheap code path.

191
00:13:00,134 --> 00:13:03,904
It is a few milliseconds and
is rounding error at off cost.

192
00:13:04,604 --> 00:13:09,274
In an agentic system, a retry
reruns the model reasoning.

193
00:13:09,734 --> 00:13:13,534
This is the most expensive thing
you do, and it does not stop there.

194
00:13:14,064 --> 00:13:19,324
The reasoning step can decide on its
own to create more work, to spawn

195
00:13:19,324 --> 00:13:21,864
another agent, to call another tool.

196
00:13:22,424 --> 00:13:23,754
So this is the difference.

197
00:13:23,884 --> 00:13:26,794
A-autonomy in the feature we want.

198
00:13:26,994 --> 00:13:31,354
It is also exactly what turns a
small, boring dependency failure

199
00:13:31,744 --> 00:13:35,594
into a big budget event, so
the fixes cannot be generic.

200
00:13:35,974 --> 00:13:37,364
They have to be agent aware

201
00:13:38,364 --> 00:13:40,044
So let us talk fixes, right?

202
00:13:40,174 --> 00:13:43,564
Fix one is the one we most wish we had.

203
00:13:43,754 --> 00:13:47,384
Like budget every request,
not a timeout, right?

204
00:13:47,954 --> 00:13:50,564
A real ceiling on the things
that actually cost money.

205
00:13:51,184 --> 00:13:55,054
Total agent turns, total
tool calls, and total spawns.

206
00:13:55,504 --> 00:13:58,904
Every request gets an allowance,
and it cannot exceed it.

207
00:13:59,324 --> 00:14:01,974
And cap fan-out depth centrally.

208
00:14:02,344 --> 00:14:06,924
An orchestrator may spawn workers,
but a worker three levels down

209
00:14:07,324 --> 00:14:09,444
does not get to spawn its own tree.

210
00:14:10,184 --> 00:14:13,644
You enforce that in one
place, not in every agent.

211
00:14:14,104 --> 00:14:17,614
And when a request hits its
budget, do not quietly keep going.

212
00:14:17,814 --> 00:14:18,914
Fail fast, right?

213
00:14:18,934 --> 00:14:21,734
Fail loud, stop, and surface it.

214
00:14:22,164 --> 00:14:26,984
Failing visibly is far better than
succeeding at 100 times the cost.

215
00:14:27,724 --> 00:14:29,554
A loud failure is a page.

216
00:14:29,704 --> 00:14:33,984
A silent overrun is a mystery, and
we already know how that ends, right?

217
00:14:34,594 --> 00:14:38,774
The next fix could be… The fix
two is about not piling on when

218
00:14:38,774 --> 00:14:41,794
something is already struggling,
like circuit breakers, right?

219
00:14:42,124 --> 00:14:46,034
When a dependency starts failing
you trip the breaker and back off

220
00:14:46,084 --> 00:14:47,634
instead of hammering it harder.

221
00:14:48,044 --> 00:14:52,094
Then you go half open, let one
request through to test recovery, and

222
00:14:52,094 --> 00:14:53,544
close it again when it is healthy.

223
00:14:54,294 --> 00:14:55,484
And collapse the layers.

224
00:14:56,034 --> 00:15:02,914
Pick exactly one layer to own retries,
the SDK or the app or the agent.

225
00:15:03,184 --> 00:15:03,584
One.

226
00:15:04,454 --> 00:15:05,684
Turn the other two off.

227
00:15:05,864 --> 00:15:10,934
No more independent triple retries
where nobody knows the other exists.

228
00:15:11,224 --> 00:15:13,404
And make repeated work idempotent.

229
00:15:14,124 --> 00:15:16,034
Give each unit of work a key.

230
00:15:16,434 --> 00:15:20,384
If it runs again, it returns the
cached result instead of paying

231
00:15:20,394 --> 00:15:21,504
to do the whole thing over.

232
00:15:21,504 --> 00:15:28,804
A retry should be cheap, and idempotency
should make it cheaper On to the next.

233
00:15:28,854 --> 00:15:32,714
The fix three is the one that would
have saved us the entire ordeal.

234
00:15:33,204 --> 00:15:35,424
Make cost a first-class signal.

235
00:15:36,424 --> 00:15:39,434
We treat latency as a
tign- a signal typically.

236
00:15:39,814 --> 00:15:41,514
We treat errors as a signal.

237
00:15:41,734 --> 00:15:43,184
We put them on the dashboard.

238
00:15:43,184 --> 00:15:46,254
We alert when there is an
error or there is a latency.

239
00:15:46,584 --> 00:15:47,554
We page on them.

240
00:15:48,104 --> 00:15:51,214
But cost, we look at
once a month on a bill.

241
00:15:51,784 --> 00:15:53,484
That gap is the whole story.

242
00:15:53,614 --> 00:15:55,864
So three moves, right?

243
00:15:56,234 --> 00:16:00,984
Emit tokens and tool calls per
request as a live metric right

244
00:16:01,014 --> 00:16:02,884
next to the latency and errors.

245
00:16:03,164 --> 00:16:05,544
Alert on volume, not just failures.

246
00:16:05,764 --> 00:16:11,004
So a request that fans out into thousands
of calls, pages someone and trace the full

247
00:16:11,024 --> 00:16:15,254
agent tree, so you can see the fan out
instead of a wall of individual calls.

248
00:16:15,644 --> 00:16:20,944
Any one of these on its own would
have caught us in minutes instead

249
00:16:20,944 --> 00:16:26,124
of the next morning So here's
how-- what we instrumented, right?

250
00:16:26,454 --> 00:16:28,474
The fixed tree sounds nice in principle.

251
00:16:28,584 --> 00:16:31,694
Here's what is-- what it
meant in practice concretely.

252
00:16:32,294 --> 00:16:36,844
So you can go build it on a Monday, right?

253
00:16:37,194 --> 00:16:39,574
One, per request cost tags.

254
00:16:39,964 --> 00:16:43,764
We tag every single request with
the tokens it burned and the tool

255
00:16:43,824 --> 00:16:46,074
calls it made, and we emit that live.

256
00:16:46,674 --> 00:16:50,214
The same way we emit latency and
errors, not in a monthly bill.

257
00:16:50,834 --> 00:16:56,074
In the moment two spans across the
agent tree, one trace per request,

258
00:16:56,114 --> 00:16:59,034
and that trace spans the orchestrator.

259
00:16:59,034 --> 00:17:04,094
Every s-sub-agent it spawned and every
tool call underneath gets tracked.

260
00:17:04,674 --> 00:17:08,514
Now, the fan-out is a picture
you can see, not a number smeared

261
00:17:08,524 --> 00:17:10,184
across dozens of services.

262
00:17:10,734 --> 00:17:13,343
And the three is the
alert on volume, right?

263
00:17:13,544 --> 00:17:17,913
You configured the traces, now you alert
on the volume, not just the errors.

264
00:17:18,243 --> 00:17:21,983
We page when a single request uses
the ceiling on calls or tokens,

265
00:17:22,304 --> 00:17:26,283
so a request that retries its way
to a success can no longer hide.

266
00:17:26,633 --> 00:17:29,844
Any of these would have
caught the storm in minutes

267
00:17:30,844 --> 00:17:31,944
Coming to the playbook.

268
00:17:32,374 --> 00:17:34,264
So here is the whole thing on one slide.

269
00:17:34,684 --> 00:17:38,664
The checklist we wish someone had
handed us before we shipped, right?

270
00:17:38,704 --> 00:17:44,044
Budget every request, max turns,
tool calls, and spawns, and fail

271
00:17:44,053 --> 00:17:45,494
loud when you hit the ceiling.

272
00:17:45,914 --> 00:17:49,924
Cap fan-out depth with one
central limit enforced everywhere.

273
00:17:50,623 --> 00:17:54,593
Own retries in exactly one
layer and turn the others off.

274
00:17:55,643 --> 00:17:58,643
Add circuit breakers on
every external dependency.

275
00:17:59,084 --> 00:18:03,104
Make repeated work idempotent
retry is cheap, right?

276
00:18:03,384 --> 00:18:08,523
And put cost tokens and calls per request
on the dashboard right next to latency,

277
00:18:08,854 --> 00:18:13,304
so that the-- you get-- you see the
errors and pick the errors fast, right?

278
00:18:13,933 --> 00:18:18,474
And none of these exact… Together,
they are difference between a system

279
00:18:18,474 --> 00:18:23,903
that fails loudly for a few dollars and
one that succeeds quietly for thousands.

280
00:18:24,264 --> 00:18:30,754
So So here's what we, w-what we
want you to walk out with, right?

281
00:18:30,844 --> 00:18:34,724
Agents do not f-fall over
the way old systems do.

282
00:18:35,054 --> 00:18:37,174
They do not crash and page you.

283
00:18:37,184 --> 00:18:40,074
They get quietly, enthusiastically busy.

284
00:18:40,454 --> 00:18:45,284
They work hard all night on nothing, and
they hand you the bill in the morning.

285
00:18:45,674 --> 00:18:49,634
The good news is that
the fix is not exotic.

286
00:18:50,064 --> 00:18:55,964
Give your agents a budget, give them
a limit, and give them a witness that

287
00:18:56,084 --> 00:19:00,734
something is watching the cost the
way you watch latency and errors.

288
00:19:01,694 --> 00:19:04,643
We built this the hard
way, so you do not have to.

289
00:19:05,013 --> 00:19:08,223
I'm Karthik, and we also have Yamini here.

290
00:19:08,583 --> 00:19:14,844
So today, before you leave, go check
what your agents are doing at two AM

291
00:19:15,214 --> 00:19:19,574
because if you're not watching the
storm is already watching your budget.

292
00:19:20,164 --> 00:19:22,754
Thank you everybody for
listening this session.

